{"command":"tts","scope":"single","createdAt":"2026-06-15T18:24:36.993Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-14T17:25:08.940Z","items":[{"input":"At 8:05 a.m. on March 4, 2026, Dr. Anaïs O’Connor-Smith arrived at St. John’s Avenue, Suite No. 4B, carrying three and a half pounds of bass and a worn résumé.\n\n“Did the lead engineer lead the review,” asked Xiǎomí Nguyen, “or was the lead pipe made of lead? Should I wind the clock before the wind rises, polish the Polish table, refuse the refuse, and record a record?”\n\nProfessor Jean-Luc Picard, Jr., Ph.D., looked up from his notes. “The meeting begins at noon, not midnight. The budget is $1,234,567.89, unless the London office meant £1,234,567.89. Call extension 42 by 5:30 p.m., Central time.”\n\nMaría-José entered with a tray and said, “¡Buenos días! Ça va? Grüß Gott.” She offered everyone croissants, jalapeño bagels, pho, gyros, açai bowls, bruschetta, and quinoa salad.\n\n“Do not overact this sentence,” Anaïs said calmly. “Do not underact this one!” Then she paused. “And please do not read every ellipsis… like… a tragic… farewell.”\n\nThe day’s exercises were simple: read “read,” “reed,” and “red”; explain whether “No. 6” means number six or no six; distinguish “US” from “us”; decide whether “invalid” means not valid or a person who is ill; and make “the dove dove into the bushes” sound natural.\n\nThen came the pronunciation list: Worcestershire, hyperbole, epitome, cache, niche, solder, colonel, segue, synecdoche, Saoirse Ronan, Chiwetel Ejiofor, Ioan Gruffudd, Ncuti Gatwa, Björk, and Sean Bean.\n\nAt 2:03 p.m., the chairman read the final line aloud: “A good narrator knows when to pause, when to emphasize, when to spell, when to pronounce, and when to admit that text alone is ambiguous.”\n","outputDir":"docs/benchmarks/tts/2026-06-15_18-24-36-993_tts-hard","status":"incomplete","metadata":{"tts":[{"ttsService":"elevenlabs","ttsModel":"eleven_v3","speaker":"hpp4J3VqNfWAUOO0d1Us","processingTime":77809,"audioFileName":"speech-e